 **MLE Assumptions:**\n  1. The model is correctly specified.\n  2. The observations are independent.\n  3. Sufficient sample size for reliable estimation.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nANOVA is applicable when comparing means across multiple groups, particularly when the data meets the assumptions of normality and homogeneity of variances. MLE is used when estimating parameters of complex models, especially when the distribution of the data is known or can be assumed.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\n- **ANOVA Procedure:**\n  1. Calculate group means and overall mean.\n  2. Compute the between-group and within-group variances.\n  3. Use the F-statistic to test the null hypothesis that all group means are equal.\n  \n- **MLE Procedure:**\n  1. Specify the likelihood function based on the chosen model.\n  2. Differentiate the log-likelihood function with respect to the parameters.\n  3. Solve the resulting equations to find parameter estimates.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- **ANOVA:**\n  - Efficient for comparing multiple groups simultaneously.\n  - Controls Type I error rate better than multiple t-tests.\n  \n- **MLE:**\n  - Provides asymptotically unbiased and efficient estimates.\n  - Flexible for a wide range of models and distributions.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- **ANOVA:**\n  - Sensitive to violations of assumptions, particularly normality and homogeneity of variances.\n  - Cannot indicate which specific groups are different without further testing (e.g., post-hoc tests).\n  \n- **MLE:**\n  - Requires correct model specification; misspecification can lead to biased estimates.\n  - Computationally intensive for complex models.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nANOVA is related to other methods such as t-tests for two groups, MANOVA for multiple dependent variables, and ANCOVA for adjusting covariates. MLE is related to Bayesian estimation and other parameter estimation techniques.\n\n**Practical Impleme